  "question": {
    "text": "If the solution $y=y(x)$ of the differential equation $\\left(x^4+2 x^3+3 x^2+2 x+2\\right) \\mathrm{d} y-\\left(2 x^2+2 x+3\\right) \\mathrm{d} x=0$ satisfies $y(-1)=-\\frac{\\pi}{4}$, then $y(0)$ is equal to :",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "YYgOE",
      "text": "$\\frac{\\pi}{2}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "7rvGA",
      "text": "$-\\frac{\\pi}{2}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "aSmg7",
      "text": "0",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "V5zkP",
      "text": "$\\frac{\\pi}{4}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "$\\begin{aligned} & \\int d y=\\int \\frac{\\left(2 x^2+2 x+3\\right)}{x^4+2 x^3+3 x^2+2 x+2} d x \\\\ & y=\\int \\frac{\\left(2 x^2+2 x+3\\right)}{\\left(x^2+1\\right)\\left(x^2+2 x+2\\right)} d x \\\\ & y=\\int \\frac{d x}{x^2+2 x+2}+\\int \\frac{d x}{x^2+1} \\\\ & y=\\tan ^{-1}(x+1)+\\tan ^{-1} x+C \\\\ & y(-1)=\\frac{-\\pi}{4} \\\\ & \\frac{-\\pi}{4}=0-\\frac{\\pi}{4}+C \\Rightarrow C=0 \\\\ & \\Rightarrow y=\\tan ^{-1}(x+1)+\\tan ^{-1} x \\\\ & y(0)=\\tan ^{-1} 1=\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\end{aligned}$",
    "image": null
  },
